            "Oxygen is a <strong>drug</strong>: it is prescribed with a device, a flow rate and a <strong>target saturation</strong>, signed for on the drug chart at each round, and titrated to the target — <strong>94–98 per cent</strong> for most acutely ill adults, <strong>88–92 per cent</strong> for those at risk of hypercapnic respiratory failure (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, severe kyphoscoliosis, neuromuscular disease, morbid obesity), in whom high concentrations can suppress the hypoxic drive and worsen the matching of ventilation and blood flow until carbon dioxide rises, the patient becomes drowsy and then stops breathing. In an <strong>emergency</strong> — collapse, shock, severe asthma, sepsis, anaphylaxis, the saturation below 85 — the nurse gives high-flow oxygen through a reservoir mask at 15 litres <strong>first</strong>, without waiting for a prescription, and the prescription and the titration follow; hypoxia kills in minutes and hypercapnia in hours. Oxygen does not treat breathlessness that is not hypoxic, and a breathless patient with a saturation of 97 per cent needs a cause found, not a mask.",
            "The <strong>devices</strong>: <strong>nasal cannulae</strong> at <strong>1–4 L/min</strong> give about 24–40 per cent (each litre adds roughly four per cent), are comfortable, allow eating and talking, are the device for the long-term and the stable, and dry and ulcerate the nostrils above 4 L/min; the prongs curve down into the nostrils, the tubing goes over the ears and under the chin, and the ears and the nares are checked for pressure damage every shift and padded. The <strong>simple face mask</strong> at <strong>5–10 L/min</strong> gives about 40–60 per cent and is <strong>never run below 5 L/min</strong>, because exhaled carbon dioxide then collects in the mask and is rebreathed. The <strong>non-rebreather reservoir mask</strong> at <strong>10–15 L/min</strong> gives 60–90 per cent: the bag is filled by occluding the valve with a finger before the mask is applied, and the flow is set so that the bag never collapses by more than a third on inspiration; it is the mask of the critically ill and the first minutes of any emergency, and a patient who still needs it after an hour needs a doctor and probably a higher level of care. The <strong>Venturi mask</strong> gives a fixed, exact concentration — 24, 28, 35, 40 or 60 per cent — through a colour-coded adaptor at the flow printed on it, and is the device for the patient who needs a controlled concentration because of the risk of carbon dioxide retention. A <strong>head box</strong> gives oxygen to an infant, nasal prongs at 0.5–2 L/min to a child, and the saturation target for children is 94 per cent and above except in cyanotic heart disease.",
