          alt: "The medication round: the rights and the three checks. Right patient — two identifiers: ask the patient to state full name and date of birth; check the wristband and the chart; never the bed number, never 'Are you Mr Mensah?'. Right drug — three checks of the label: taking it from the shelf, preparing it, before giving or returning; look-alike sound-alike names. Right dose — calculate, and have high-alert and paediatric doses checked independently by a second nurse. Right route. Right time — within 30 minutes; keep antibiotic intervals; with or without food as required. Right documentation — sign immediately after the dose, never before. Also: right reason, right response, right to refuse, right education. Before giving: allergy box; last dose; parameters — pulse before digoxin, blood pressure before antihypertensives, respiratory rate before opioids, blood glucose before insulin, potassium before potassium; the patient told what and why. Do not give: illegible, incomplete or unclear prescription; dose outside the usual range; allergy; parameters out of range; a drug you cannot explain — withhold, contact the prescriber, document.",
          caption: "Two identifiers spoken by the patient, three reads of the label, a second checker for the dangerous ones, the parameter measured before the dose, and the signature after it. The dose that cannot be explained is withheld and the prescriber called.",
