          alt: "Injections: site, needle, angle and volume. Subcutaneous: abdomen 5 cm from the umbilicus, outer upper arm, anterior thigh; 25 to 27 gauge, 4 to 8 mm needle; 45 to 90 degrees depending on needle length and fat; up to 1 mL; no aspiration; rotate sites — insulin, heparin, enoxaparin, some vaccines; do not massage heparin. Intramuscular: ventrogluteal — heel of the hand on the greater trochanter, index finger to the anterior superior iliac spine, middle finger along the iliac crest, inject in the V — preferred in adults, up to 3 mL; deltoid — 2 to 3 finger-widths below the acromion, up to 1 mL, vaccines; vastus lateralis — middle third of the outer thigh, infants and children, up to 1 to 2 mL; dorsogluteal — upper outer quadrant, avoided: sciatic nerve; 21 to 23 gauge, 25 to 38 mm; 90 degrees; Z-track for irritant and staining drugs — iron, some antipsychotics. Intradermal: inner forearm, 10 to 15 degrees, bevel up, 0.1 mL raising a bleb — BCG, Mantoux, allergy testing. Intravenous: into a cannula or port after checking patency and flushing; at the prescribed rate; vesicants and extravasation. Always: hand hygiene; a new sterile needle and syringe for every injection; check the vial and expiry; no recapping; sharps box at the point of use.",
