            "Every injection begins with hand hygiene, the prescription, the right patient and a <strong>new sterile needle and syringe</strong> — one needle, one syringe, one patient, one time — and ends with the needle in a sharps box at the point of use, unrecapped, and a signature. Drawing up: the ampoule tapped to bring the fluid down, the neck snapped away from the body with a gauze, and the drug drawn through a filter or blunt needle to avoid glass; the <strong>vial</strong> checked for name, strength, expiry, clarity and particles, its rubber top cleaned with alcohol and allowed to dry, air equal to the volume required injected first, and a multidose vial dated on opening and discarded at the interval the label and the policy set (and never entered with a used needle); a powder reconstituted with the stated diluent and volume and rolled rather than shaken; the needle changed after drawing up through rubber or from a glass ampoule; and the dose checked by a second nurse where required. The patient is told what is being given and why, positioned to relax the muscle, and the site inspected and chosen by the volume, the viscosity and the patient's tissue, and <strong>rotated</strong>.",
            "<strong>Intradermal</strong> injections — BCG, the Mantoux test, allergy testing — go into the dermis of the inner forearm or the deltoid region with a 25–27 gauge short needle, <strong>bevel up at 10–15 degrees</strong>, 0.1 mL raising a pale <strong>bleb</strong>; no massage. <strong>Subcutaneous</strong> injections — insulin, heparin and enoxaparin, some vaccines, morphine in palliative care — go into the fat of the abdomen (5 cm away from the umbilicus), the outer upper arm or the anterior thigh with a 25–27 gauge needle of 4–8 mm (longer needles in the obese) at <strong>90 degrees</strong> with a short needle or <strong>45 degrees</strong> with a longer one or in a thin patient, a skin fold lifted if needed, up to 1 mL, <strong>no aspiration</strong>, and no massage after heparin, which bruises; sites are rotated systematically to prevent lipohypertrophy, which makes insulin absorption erratic. <strong>Intramuscular</strong> injections — most vaccines, antibiotics, analgesics, antiemetics, oxytocin, iron, depot preparations — go into muscle with a 21–23 gauge needle of 25–38 mm (longer in the obese; a needle that reaches only fat gives a subcutaneous dose and an abscess) at <strong>90 degrees</strong>, with the skin stretched or bunched as the site dictates; the <strong>ventrogluteal</strong> site — the heel of the hand on the greater trochanter, the index finger on the anterior superior iliac spine, the middle finger along the iliac crest, the injection in the V between the fingers — is the preferred adult site, free of major nerves and vessels and taking up to 3 mL; the <strong>deltoid</strong>, two to three finger-widths below the acromion, takes up to 1 mL and is the vaccine site in adults and older children; the <strong>vastus lateralis</strong>, the middle third of the outer thigh, is the site for infants and small children (up to 1 mL in an infant, 2 mL in a child) and an alternative in adults (up to 3 mL); the <strong>dorsogluteal</strong> upper outer quadrant is <strong>avoided</strong> wherever possible because of the sciatic nerve and the thick fat over it, and never used in infants. Aspiration before intramuscular injection is no longer recommended for vaccines and is a matter of policy for other drugs; the <strong>Z-track</strong> technique — the skin pulled 2–3 cm to one side before the needle enters and released after it is withdrawn so that the track seals — is used for irritant and staining drugs such as iron dextran and for depot antipsychotics. The injection is given slowly (about 1 mL per ten seconds), the needle withdrawn smoothly and gentle pressure applied with dry gauze; the site, the drug, the dose and the patient's response are recorded. <strong>Intravenous</strong> drugs are given by nurses trained and assessed to do so, into a cannula whose patency has been confirmed by a saline flush, at the rate the drug's monograph specifies (a bolus too fast is the mechanism of death for potassium, many antibiotics, phenytoin and magnesium), diluted as the monograph says, with compatibility checked before anything is added to a running line, the line flushed between drugs, and the patient watched for the first minutes for anaphylaxis — which is treated with intramuscular adrenaline 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh, repeated at five minutes, with the infusion stopped, the airway supported, oxygen, legs raised and the emergency team called.",
