          alt: "Insulin: the safety rules. Always an insulin syringe marked in units — never a 1 mL tuberculin syringe, never a 2 mL syringe, never 'U' written for units. Check the type: rapid, short, intermediate, long, mixed — same name, different action; two nurses check the prescription, the vial and the drawn-up dose. Capillary glucose before every dose; withhold and call if the patient is hypoglycaemic or nil by mouth; a meal must follow a rapid or short-acting dose within the time the preparation requires. Cloudy insulin: roll gently, never shake; draw up clear before cloudy when mixing. Subcutaneous: abdomen, thigh, upper arm; rotate within a site; 90 degrees with a 4 to 6 mm needle, no aspiration. Store unopened vials in the fridge at 2 to 8 degrees Celsius, never frozen; the vial in use at room temperature for up to 28 days, dated. Hypoglycaemia — sweating, tremor, hunger, confusion, drowsiness, convulsions: if conscious 15 to 20 g of fast sugar, recheck in 15 minutes, then a longer-acting carbohydrate; if unconscious nothing by mouth — intravenous dextrose or intramuscular glucagon and call.",
